    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GPUs that share the same index across different servers are interconnected via the same leaf switch.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The peak unidirectional Device-to-Device (D2D), and pinned Device-to-Host (D2H) (and vice versa) bandwidths on each GPU</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">are in GPU memory at the same time while all other activations are offloaded.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Heterogeneous computing involves using a combination of different types of processors and accelerators within a single system to perform computational tasks.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Each GPU is responsible for updating specific shard of the model parameters.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Memory capacity of g i</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the compute capability of each GPU is denoted as Speed</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the number of GPUs gets larger, because it eliminates communication over all GPUs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">APIs for extracting/loading model state to/from GPUs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">across all GPUs to fit an LLM into multiple GPUs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">high-end GPUs such as A100 and H100 are difficult to aquire, while mid-tier GPUs (A10G, 2 A minibatch is split in microbatches. V100, T4) have higher but still limited and dynamic availability.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Users submit training jobs with multi-dimensional parallelism to a DL job scheduler [ 38 , 69 ], which allocates it to GPUs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">typically fit in the memory of a single GPU.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">It needs a large number of GPU-accelerators to train these large-scale models.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the optimizer caps GPU memory usage at 80%.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the original GPU allocation of a job may change over time [ 69 ] for several reasons</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">When a subset of GPUs fail in a given model parallel partition, NTP redistributes the work to remaining GPUs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Example accelerator devices used to train DNNs are GPUs</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
